---
title: BoosterTutor, Before the Words
blurb: The draft input is done; now I'm making myself design the Claude layer before I build any of it.
date: 2026-10-15
tags: [web]
---

BoosterTutor just wrapped up its third phase of development, which means the part I'm most excited
about is next: getting Claude to coherently explain a draft. Before I get there, though,
I want to write down where things landed, because a lot changed in the last few days
and some of it changed my mind about what v1 will even be.

Quick refresher if you haven't seen the [project page](/projects/booster-tutor): the
whole app rests on one rule. Python computes every fact and pick-verdict, while the LLM
only explains them. If you ask a language model whether or not a pick was good, it will tell
you, *confidently*, and you would have no idea where that answer came from. If you hand it the
answer and ask it to explain, however, it can be genuinely useful.

## One way in

Phase 3 was about getting a real draft into the system and running the analysis on it.
I ended up cutting the inputs down to exactly one: a 17Lands draft link. You paste it,
the backend fetches that single draft's pick log and event info from 17Lands, and
everything else happens on my side. Arena's `Player.log` input is pushed back until the rest
of v1 exists, and I nixed MTGO support altogether. I believe the 17Lands link already covers 
most of the people who'd actually care about a tool like this.

That fetch needed its own decision record, since I'd previously written down that
BoosterTutor never calls 17lands.com at all. That rule was really about their aggregate
card stats, which they publish as public datasets and would rather you not scrape. A
single user's draft is a different thing. It isn't in the public data, and it only
exists to us at the moment someone hands over the link. So the exception is narrow:
only on an explicit submission, one fetch per draft, never in bulk.

## Reading the wheel

The other big piece was wheel analysis. In a draft, the pack you open comes back to you
eight picks later, minus whatever the other seven players took. That's a surprisingly
rich signal. If the green cards keep coming back, green is probably open. If the good
red cards vanish every lap, someone next to you is most likely in red.

I used to try to fold this into the fitness score as an "openness" term, but it didn't work at all. 
I rewrote it, then restricted it to pack one, but ultimately a backtest over 43,650
real drafts fit its weight to exactly zero. Now it's retired as a score, and the wheel
now just reports facts: what came back, what the other players at the table took, and whether those cards
were any good. "Good" here means at or above the format's measured average win rate,
which I like because it's a number the data hands me rather than a threshold I made up.
Claude can say "a color is flowing" later, but it's really this script's job to determine that.

## Designing Phase 4 before building it

Phase 4 is the Claude integration step, and I've deliberately stopped myself from writing
any of it yet. The plan for it was written back in August, before most of the decisions
above, and a few things no longer line up.

The biggest one is what a review says when nothing went wrong. Under the current
grading rules, only a `mistake` earns a written note. I ran a real Hobbit draft through
the pipeline and it came back with 34 solid picks, 5 indifferent ones, 3 questionable,
and *zero* mistakes. That sounds like a perfectly reasonable draft! But the old plan assumed a blurb
for every pick, batched into a call per pack plus a summary, and now I'm not sure any of
that batching is what I actually want. I need to figure out what the review should actually tell
someone who drafted well, which is what I'll do today.

There's also some plumbing issues. The analysis only knows cards by their oracle IDs, so
something has to look up the names, mana costs and rarity before a prompt gets built, and
that step needs a sensible home between the pure analysis code and everything that
touches the database. I also want an eval that fails if Claude ever writes a number that
wasn't in its brief, which means I have to be careful about how numbers get formatted
going in. If the brief says 2.1pp and Claude says 2%, is that invented? I'd rather
decide that now than argue with a test later.

Finally, I recently decided that anonymous visitors get the full Python analysis but
no Claude calls at all, and accounts don't arrive until Phase 6. So Phase 4 is probably a
module plus an eval harness with no user-facing endpoint, which honestly feels right.
 I'll write the next one once that design is settled.
